**Key idea:** for a **fixed** latency, compare with `$past(in_data, N)`. For a **stallable** pipeline, use the gated form `$past(x, N, en)`. For a **variable** latency, capture the input in a **local variable** at the antecedent and compare when the output appears.

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "clk",       "wave": "p......" },
  { "name": "in_valid",  "wave": "01..0.." },
  { "name": "in_data",   "wave": "x===x..", "data": ["A", "B", "C"] },
  { "name": "out_valid", "wave": "0..1..0" },
  { "name": "out_data",  "wave": "x..===x", "data": ["A+1", "B+1", "C+1"] }
],
  "head": { "text": "2-cycle pipeline: out_data == $past(in_data, 2) + 1 whenever out_valid", "tick": 0 } }
```

### Code
```systemverilog
// 1) Fixed latency of 2
a_lat2 : assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
           out_valid |-> out_data == $past(in_data, 2) + 1);

// 2) Pipeline that only advances when en is high: count only enabled clocks
a_stall : assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
           (en && out_valid) |-> out_data == $past(in_data, 2, en) + 1);

// 3) Variable latency (1..8 cycles), in-order, one outstanding at a time
property p_var_lat;
  logic [31:0] v;
  @(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
    (in_valid, v = in_data) |-> ##[1:8] (out_valid && out_data == v + 1);
endproperty
a_var : assert property (p_var_lat);
```

| Situation | Tool |
| --- | --- |
| fixed latency, no stalls | `$past(x, N)` |
| fixed number of *enabled* stages | `$past(x, N, en)` |
| variable latency | local variable captured at the antecedent |
| many outstanding, reordering | aux model / scoreboard |

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- In the first N cycles after reset `$past` returns pre-reset (or initial) values: guard with `$past(rst_n, N)` or rely on `disable iff` plus valid qualifiers.
- `$past(x, N)` for large N builds an N-deep shift register in formal: fine for small N.

### Follow-ups
- **Why not `##2` in the consequent?** `in_valid |-> ##2 out_data == in_data + 1` compares with the sampled `in_data` *two cycles later*, not the captured value. Use `$past` or a local variable.

**Key idea:** in a property, `req |-> ##[1:$] ack` is **weak**: if simulation ends while the attempt is still waiting for `ack`, it is *not* a failure, so a hang never fails. Make the obligation **strong** (`strong(...)` or `s_eventually`) so an unfinished attempt fails at end of sim; better still, bound it.

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "clk", "wave": "p.......|" },
  { "name": "req", "wave": "010.....|" },
  { "name": "ack", "wave": "0.......|" },
  { "name": "weak ##[1:$] ack",   "wave": "x=......|", "data": ["pending: passes at end of sim"] },
  { "name": "strong(##[1:$] ack)", "wave": "x=......|", "data": ["pending: FAILS at end of sim"] }
],
  "head": { "text": "ack never comes: only the strong form reports the hang" } }
```

### Code
```systemverilog
// Weak (default): a hang goes unnoticed
a_weak    : assert property (@(posedge clk) req |-> ##[1:$] ack);

// Strong: unfinished attempts fail when the simulation ends
a_strong  : assert property (@(posedge clk) req |-> strong(##[1:$] ack));
a_s_event : assert property (@(posedge clk) req |-> s_eventually ack);

// Preferred in practice: a bounded safety check that fails at the cycle it is late
a_bounded : assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n) req |-> ##[1:64] ack);
```

| Form | Sim ends while waiting | Formal |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `##[1:$] ack` (weak) | pass | not a liveness check |
| `strong(##[1:$] ack)` / `s_eventually` | **fail** | liveness: needs fairness, harder proof |
| `##[1:N] ack` | fail at cycle N | bounded safety: easy to prove |

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Strong properties need the test to end *cleanly*: an early `$finish` while legal traffic is outstanding produces false failures.
- Unbounded ranges create many threads; bounded windows are cheaper.

### Follow-ups
- **Why prefer the bounded form?** It reports the failure at the exact cycle, works in formal as safety, and documents the latency spec.

**Key idea:** the AXI handshake rule is "once valid, hold valid and payload until the transfer". A stall cycle is `valid && !ready`; the cycle after a stall must still have `valid` and the same `data`. Add an X check and covers so the rule is not vacuous.

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "clk",   "wave": "p...." },
  { "name": "valid", "wave": "01..0" },
  { "name": "ready", "wave": "0..10", "node": "...a" },
  { "name": "data",  "wave": "x=..x", "data": ["D (held stable)"] }
],
  "head": { "text": "stall in cycles 1-2: valid and data must hold; transfer at cycle 3", "tick": 0 } }
```

### Code
```systemverilog
module vr_sva #(parameter int W = 32) (
  input logic         clk, rst_n,
  input logic         valid, ready,
  input logic [W-1:0] data
);
  default clocking cb @(posedge clk); endclocking
  default disable iff (!rst_n);

  // 1. Once valid is asserted it can't drop until ready
  a_vld_hold : assert property (valid && !ready |=> valid)
    else $error("[%0t] valid dropped before ready", $time);
  // 2. Payload stable while stalled
  a_dat_hold : assert property (valid && !ready |=> $stable(data))
    else $error("[%0t] data changed during stall", $time);
  // 3. No X on the payload when valid
  a_no_x     : assert property (valid |-> !$isunknown(data));
  // 4. valid itself never X out of reset
  a_vld_known: assert property (!$isunknown(valid));

  // Covers: prove stalls and back-to-back transfers actually happened
  c_stall    : cover property (valid && !ready ##1 valid && ready);
  c_b2b      : cover property (valid && ready ##1 valid && ready);
endmodule

bind my_dut vr_sva #(.W(32)) u_vr_sva (.clk(clk), .rst_n(rst_n),
                                      .valid(m_valid), .ready(m_ready), .data(m_data));
```

| Assertion | Catches |
| --- | --- |
| `a_vld_hold` | source withdraws a transfer |
| `a_dat_hold` | payload changes mid-stall |
| `a_no_x` | garbage on the bus when valid |
| covers | vacuous checks (never stalled) |

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- `|=>` not `|->`: the rule is about the *next* cycle after a stall.
- AXI also says the source must not wait for `ready` before asserting `valid` (deadlock): `ready` may depend on `valid`, never the reverse. That's a liveness/design property, not a simple assertion.
- If the DUT is the receiver, these become **assumes** in formal.

### Follow-ups
- **Ready must eventually come?** `valid |-> s_eventually ready` or bounded `##[0:N] ready`.

**Key idea:** two kinds of rules. **Interface rules**: nobody writes when `full` or reads when `empty`. **Design rules**: flags are consistent with an occupancy model (`full` iff count == DEPTH, `empty` iff count == 0). Put them in a checker module and attach it with `bind`.

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "clk",   "wave": "p......." },
  { "name": "wr_en", "wave": "01...0.." },
  { "name": "rd_en", "wave": "0.....10" },
  { "name": "count", "wave": "=.====.=", "data": ["0", "1", "2", "3", "4", "3"] },
  { "name": "full",  "wave": "0....1.0" },
  { "name": "empty", "wave": "1.0....." }
],
  "head": { "text": "DEPTH = 4: full in cycles 5-6; a wr_en there would be an overflow", "tick": 0 } }
```

### Code
```systemverilog
module fifo_sva #(parameter int DEPTH = 16) (
  input logic clk, rst_n, wr_en, rd_en, full, empty
);
  default clocking @(posedge clk); endclocking
  default disable iff (!rst_n);

  // Interface rules (become assumes on the inputs in formal)
  a_no_ovf : assert property (full  |-> !wr_en) else $error("write when full");
  a_no_udf : assert property (empty |-> !rd_en) else $error("read when empty");
  a_excl   : assert property (!(full && empty));

  // Design rules against a reference occupancy counter
  int unsigned cnt;
  always_ff @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n)
    if (!rst_n) cnt <= 0;
    else        cnt <= cnt + (wr_en && !full) - (rd_en && !empty);

  a_full_ok  : assert property (full  == (cnt == DEPTH));
  a_empty_ok : assert property (empty == (cnt == 0));
  a_rst_empty: assert property ($rose(rst_n) |-> empty);

  // Coverage of the interesting corners
  c_full       : cover property ($rose(full));
  c_full2empty : cover property (full ##[1:$] empty);
  c_rw_same    : cover property (wr_en && rd_en && !full && !empty);
endmodule

// Attach to every fifo instance, RTL untouched
bind fifo fifo_sva #(.DEPTH(DEPTH)) u_fifo_sva (.*);
```

| Assertion | Bug it catches |
| --- | --- |
| `a_no_ovf` / `a_no_udf` | upstream/downstream ignores flags |
| `a_excl` | corrupted flag logic |
| `a_full_ok` / `a_empty_ok` | off-by-one in pointer compare |
| `a_rst_empty` | flags not cleared by reset |

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- If the spec says "write when full is ignored", replace `a_no_ovf` with "count does not change on a write when full".
- `a_rst_empty` must not be disabled by the same reset it checks; `$rose(rst_n)` is evaluated after reset releases, so it works.

### Follow-ups
- **Async FIFO?** Clock each rule in its own domain; flags are pessimistic (full may lag), so check "never overflow", not exact equality.

**Key idea:** these composition operators express "while", "inside of", "only the first one" and "has just happened" without writing the expansions by hand.

| Operator | Meaning | Typical use |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `e throughout s` | expression e holds on every cycle of s | valid stays high for the whole burst |
| `s1 within s2` | s1 matches somewhere inside s2's span | an interrupt is raised during the transfer |
| `first_match(s)` | only the earliest match of s | trim threads from ranges in antecedents |
| `s.triggered` | s ended in the current cycle | build properties from named events |
| `not p` | property negation | "this must never happen" |

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "clk",   "wave": "p......." },
  { "name": "start", "wave": "010....." },
  { "name": "beat",  "wave": "0.1.01.0" },
  { "name": "valid", "wave": "01.....0" },
  { "name": "valid throughout beat[->4]", "wave": "0.=...=0", "data": ["checking", "pass"] }
],
  "head": { "text": "4 beats at cycles 2,3,5,6; valid must be high every cycle until the 4th beat", "tick": 0 } }
```

### Code
```systemverilog
// valid must stay high for the whole 4-beat burst
a_burst : assert property (@(posedge clk) start |=> valid throughout beat[->4]);

// unbounded range in an antecedent: keep only the first gnt
a_busy  : assert property (@(posedge clk) first_match(req ##[1:$] gnt) |=> busy);

// irq must occur during a DMA transfer window
c_irq   : cover property (@(posedge clk) (irq[->1]) within ($rose(dma_on) ##1 dma_on[*1:$] ##1 !dma_on));

// .triggered: reuse a named sequence's end point
sequence s_cfg_done; @(posedge clk) cfg_wr ##1 cfg_ack; endsequence
a_go    : assert property (@(posedge clk) go |-> s_cfg_done.triggered);
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- `throughout` binds loosely: write `e throughout (s)` with parentheses when s is complex.
- Without `first_match`, `req ##[1:$] gnt |=> busy` checks `busy` after **every** later `gnt`, not just the first.
- `.triggered` replaces the old `.ended` (deprecated in 1800-2009).

### Follow-ups
- **`and` vs `intersect`?** Same start; `and` ends at the later end, `intersect` needs the same end.

**Key idea:** CDC assertions check the **protocol assumptions** that make the synchronizer safe, on the **source clock**: data held stable while the request is outstanding, request held until acknowledged, and input pulses spaced so the slow domain can see each one.

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "src_clk",  "wave": "p.........." },
  { "name": "req",      "wave": "01....0...." },
  { "name": "data",     "wave": "x=......x..", "data": ["D stable"] },
  { "name": "ack_sync", "wave": "0....1...0." },
  { "name": "phase",    "wave": "x=...=.=.=x", "data": ["1 req", "2 ack", "3 req low", "4 ack low"] }
],
  "head": { "text": "4-phase handshake seen in the source domain", "tick": 0 } }
```

### Code
```systemverilog
module cdc_hs_sva #(parameter int W = 32) (
  input logic src_clk, src_rst_n, req, ack_sync,
  input logic [W-1:0] data
);
  default clocking cb @(posedge src_clk); endclocking
  default disable iff (!src_rst_n);

  // phase 1-2: data stable and req held until ack arrives
  a_cdc_stable : assert property (req && !ack_sync |=> $stable(data));
  a_cdc_order  : assert property (req && !ack_sync |=> req);
  // phase 3-4: don't start a new request until ack has dropped
  a_cdc_rearm  : assert property (!req && ack_sync |=> !req);
  // ack only in response to a request
  a_ack_rise   : assert property ($rose(ack_sync) |-> req);
  c_full_cycle : cover  property ($rose(req) ##[1:$] $rose(ack_sync) ##[1:$] $fell(req) ##[1:$] $fell(ack_sync));
endmodule

// Pulse synchronizer: fast -> slow (ratio about 3:1, 2-flop sync needs margin)
module pulse_sync_sva (input logic fast_clk, rst_n, pulse);
  a_pulse_gap : assert property (@(posedge fast_clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
                  $rose(pulse) |=> !pulse [*4])
    else $error("[%0t] pulses too close for the slow domain", $time);
  a_pulse_1cy : assert property (@(posedge fast_clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
                  $rose(pulse) |=> !pulse);
endmodule
```

| Assertion | Protects against |
| --- | --- |
| `a_cdc_stable` | dst samples data while it changes (torn word) |
| `a_cdc_order` | request withdrawn before it was seen |
| `a_cdc_rearm` | phase skipped, lost transfer |
| `a_pulse_gap` | two toggles merged, a pulse lost |

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Each property is clocked on **one** clock; mixing `src_clk` and `dst_clk` signals in one property needs explicit multi-clock syntax and synchronized signals.
- The gap (4 here) is derived from the clock ratio plus synchronizer latency; parameterize it.
- These are protocol checks; structural CDC (sync on every crossing, no reconvergence) is a separate static check.

### Follow-ups
- **Gray-coded pointer rule?** `$countones(ptr ^ $past(ptr)) <= 1` on the source clock.
